  <h1>From a private key to a public key on a toy elliptic curve</h1>
  <p class="lead">Elliptic-curve scalar multiplication <span class="math">Q = k·G</span> is the one-way step that turns an Ethereum private key into a public key. This page does it by hand on the curve <span class="math">y² = x³ + 7 (mod 17)</span>, animates it, tests it statistically, and then repeats the same code on the real curve secp256k1.</p>
  <p><span class="badge" id="selftest">Running self-test…</span></p>
</header>

<section id="background">
  <h2>1. Background and task</h2>
  <p><b>Elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC)</b> is an approach to public-key cryptography based on the algebraic structure of elliptic curves over finite fields.</p>
  <p>A private key <span class="math">k</span> is an integer. The public key is the curve point <span class="math">Q = k·G</span>, where <span class="math">G</span> is a fixed base point published with the curve. Computing <span class="math">Q</span> from <span class="math">k</span> takes about <span class="math">log₂ k</span> curve operations. Recovering <span class="math">k</span> from <span class="math">Q</span> is the Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm Problem (ECDLP), for which no efficient method is known on a well-chosen curve.</p>
  <p>This is a one-way function, but it is not a hash function. It has a fixed input range, an algebraic structure (the group law), and its hardness comes from a number-theoretic problem. A cryptographic hash has none of those properties by design. Section 5 measures where the two behave alike and where they do not.</p>
  <div class="formula">Curve E:  y² ≡ x³ + a·x + b (mod p),   a = 0, b = 7   (secp256k1 shape)<br>Toy field: p = 17          Base point: G = (1, 5)       check: 5² = 25 ≡ 8 ≡ 1³ + 7 (mod 17)<br>Private key: k = 6         Public key: Q = 6·G</div>
</section>

<section id="addition">
  <h2>2. How two points are added</h2>
  <p>On a finite field the usual picture of a line through two points does not exist, but the algebra of the chord-and-tangent rule still works. All arithmetic is mod <span class="math">p</span>, and a division means multiplying by a modular inverse.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Addition, P ≠ R</h3>
      <div class="formula">m  = (y₂ − y₁) · (x₂ − x₁)⁻¹<br>x₃ = m² − x₁ − x₂<br>y₃ = m · (x₁ − x₃) − y₁</div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Doubling, P = R</h3>
      <div class="formula">m  = (3·x₁² + a) · (2·y₁)⁻¹<br>x₃ = m² − 2·x₁<br>y₃ = m · (x₁ − x₃) − y₁</div></div>
  </div>
  <p>Special cases: <span class="math">P + (−P) = O</span>, the point at infinity, where <span class="math">−(x, y) = (x, −y)</span>. <span class="math">O</span> is the identity. The scalar product is computed either by adding <span class="math">G</span> repeatedly (<span class="math">G, 2G, 3G, …, kG</span>) or by double-and-add, which reads the bits of <span class="math">k</span> and needs only about <span class="math">log₂ k</span> doublings plus one addition per 1-bit.</p>
</section>

