<section id="discussion">
  <h2>6. Discussion: one-way function or hash function?</h2>
  <p>Each property from the exploration is either a requirement of a cryptographic hash, a feature of the curve group, or both. The right column of the table is what the experiments above show for scalar multiplication.</p>
  <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="cmp">
    <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Cryptographic hash (Keccak-256 as control)</th><th>EC scalar multiplication k ↦ kG</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Determinism</b></td><td>Yes. The same input always gives the same digest.</td><td>Yes. The same k always gives the same Q, which is what lets a wallet rebuild its keys from a seed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Uniform-looking output</b></td><td>Design goal. Every output bit should look like a fair coin for any input.</td><td>Over a full cycle the map is a bijection onto the subgroup, so every point appears exactly once. Coordinates look scattered but are not uniform bit strings: x only takes values where x³+7 is a square, and the top bit is biased when p is not a power of two. This is a property of the group, not a goal of the design.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Bit balance</b></td><td>Required for every output bit.</td><td>Mostly balanced except the bias above. Matters little for security, since keys are used through group operations.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Avalanche</b></td><td>Required. Flipping one input bit flips about half the output bits, in every case.</td><td>Roughly as scrambled as two random points, but nobody designs for it. Neighbouring keys are algebraically linked: (k+1)G = kG + G.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Correlation of input and output</b></td><td>None should be detectable.</td><td>No linear correlation, but one exact relation is public: (n−k)G = −kG, so x repeats and y mirrors. It does not break ECDLP.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Homomorphism</b></td><td>A flaw. H(a+b) must not be computable from H(a) and H(b).</td><td>The core feature: (a+b)G = aG + bG. It is what makes signatures, ECDH and key derivation possible.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Collisions</b></td><td>Infinite inputs map to a fixed size, so collisions exist but must be infeasible to find (about 2¹²⁸ work for a 256-bit hash).</td><td>None for k in 1 … n−1. Different keys always give different points.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Where hardness comes from</b></td><td>Many rounds of mixing, no proof.</td><td>The ECDLP. Best generic attacks need about √n steps, roughly 2¹²⁸ for secp256k1, but only a few dozen for this toy curve. The toy curve is also supersingular (p ≡ 2 mod 3), a family with a known shortcut that real curves avoid; secp256k1 has p ≡ 1 mod 3.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Input size</b></td><td>Any length.</td><td>A scalar below the group order.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Conclusion. Avalanche, bit balance and absence of correlation are statistical design requirements of hash functions. For scalar multiplication they are side effects, and the property that matters is the opposite one: a rigid algebraic structure that is easy to use forwards and believed hard to invert. Statistical tests on the output can therefore not certify a public-key function. A toy curve may pass them and still fall to a trivial brute-force search, as the attack button in section 3 shows.</p>
</section>
<footer>Everything on this page runs locally in the browser. Toy curves are for learning and offer no security.</footer>
